General Model Searching Workflow
1. To prevent data leakage, all experiments involving T25 (n=34) were excluded from all model fitting steps. These experiments contained opposing monomers including T1-T16, T18. Additionally, a nested k-fold cross-validation (CV) procedure used for model training and internal validation. Nine model types were evaluated: multiple linear regression (MLR), ridge regression (Ridge), lasso regression (Lasso), Bayesian ridge regression (BRR), kernel ridge regression (KRR), support vector regression (SVR), k-nearest neighbors regression (KNN), random forest regression (RF), and XGBoost regression (XGB). The nested CV procedure used four outer splits repeated three times and four inner splits repeated three times. Within the nested CV, Bayesian optimization was conducted over 20 trials, allowing 4 to 10 selected features and a maximum pairwise feature correlation of 0.75.
2. Candidate model performance was compared using three criteria: the out-of-fold R^2, which measures predictive performance on held-out data; the out-of-fold mean absolute error (MAE), which measures the average magnitude of the prediction errors; and the difference between the in-fold and out-of-fold R^2 values, which indicates the extent of overfitting. Models were compared to maximize out-of-fold R^2, minimize out-of-fold MAE, and minimize the difference between the in-fold and out-of-fold R^2 values.
3. The held-out experiments involving T25 were used to evaluate each model and guide selection of the final model type.
4. Model performance statistics and feature interpretations were evaluated for the selected model.
5. The selected model was retrained using all experiments, including those involving T25.
6. The final model was externally tested using a completely unseen out-of-sample (OOS) dataset.

